  "Question": "Consider the sequence \\[ 1,-2,3,-4,5,-6,\\ldots, \\] whose \\(n\\)th term is \\((-1)^{n+1}n.\\) What is the average of the first \\(200\\) terms of the sequence?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(-0.5\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(0.5\\)",
    "E": "\\(1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Each positive odd term is followed by the negative of the next integer, so every consecutive pair sums to \\(-1\\):\n\\[\n1-2=-1,\\qquad 3-4=-1,\\qquad\\ldots,\\qquad199-200=-1.\n\\]\nThe first \\(200\\) terms make exactly \\(100\\) such pairs, with no terms left over. Their total is therefore \\(100(-1)=-100\\).\n\nThe average is the total divided by the number of terms:\n\\[\n\\frac{-100}{200}=-\\frac12=-0.5.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(-0.5\\), choice B.",
